detail.abstract:
Code-generating large language models have advanced rapidly from function-level synthesis to practical programming and repository-level software maintenance. Yet the scientific value of these advances depends critically on benchmark quality, evaluation rigour, and reproducibility. This paper presents a structured analytical review of the benchmark ecosystem for code-generating large language models and coding agents, with emphasis on execution-based evaluation, benchmark design quality, and cross-phase software-engineering coverage. The review synthesises foundational and recent primary sources spanning HumanEval, MBPP, APPS, MultiPL-E, DS-1000, BigCodeBench, SWE bench, and contemporary survey and benchmark-audit literature. Across these sources, the field has progressed from small Python-only tasks to multilingual, domain-specific, tool use, and repository-level benchmarks; however, current benchmark coverage remains imbalanced, with approximately 60% of surveyed benchmarks concentrated in the software-development phase and only small fractions devoted to requirements and design. Recent benchmark audits further show that nearly 70% of code-related benchmarks lacked explicit data-quality assurance measures, while many omitted key details required for reproducibility.&nbsp; 
<div>
 In response, this paper proposes a reproducibility-centred benchmark protocol for future evaluations. The protocol combines official benchmark releases, prompt canonicalisation, containerised execution, stronger execution oracles, contamination-aware reporting, and benchmark portfolios that span multiple software-development-life-cycle phases. The main conclusion is that benchmark design, not model scaling alone, has become the central bottleneck in credible research on automated code generation. Accordingly, the paper recommends a shift from single-benchmark reporting toward benchmark portfolios that jointly assess correctness, robustness, language breadth, practical tool use, and repository level maintenance. The resulting manuscript is intended to be directly adaptable for submission to a general computer-science venue.&nbsp;
</div>
